title: Deleting duplicate copies
tags: Duplicates
kind: guide
summary: Keep the copy under the cursor, trash one copy, or keep what a query matches across every group — your choice, never a heuristic's.

Which copy survives is always yours to choose: nothing picks one for you — not the
oldest, not the shortest path. You choose it copy by copy, or you write the rule and
metafolder applies it to every group.

!! One group at a time

In the [[duplicates panel]], both deletions act on the row under the cursor:

* ''keep this one'' — <<key "duplicates:keep" "p k">>, and the first item of the context menu: trashes every ''other'' copy of the group. The question names every path it will trash, and how much space pruning the trash will then reclaim.
* ''trash this one'' — <<key "item:delete" Delete>>: that copy alone, as Delete does anywhere else ([[Deleting an item]]). On a group row, Delete deletes the group's own metarecord.

Keeping one asks first, always, since it touches several files; trashing one
asks unless the question is switched off ([[Confirming a trashing]]).

!! Every group, by a query

<<cmd "mf duplicate keep">> keeps the copies a query matches: in each group with
such a copy, the others go to the trash. The query is any query — a folder,
`mfr_path ->* "/photos/sorted"`, or a field you set on the copies to keep,
`keep = true`, which a later scan leaves alone. <<cmd "mf duplicate trash">> is the
same with the complement, for a query naming what to give up — a folder to empty of
what is kept elsewhere.

What is trashed is itself a query, and each copy it holds has a twin the same run
leaves alone:

|!Command |!Copies trashed |!The twin left |
|`keep Q` |`same(mfr_duplicate_group, Q) AND NOT Q` |in `Q` |
|`trash Q` |`Q AND same(mfr_duplicate_group, NOT Q)` |outside `Q` |

So a group is touched only when the query splits it, and never loses its last copy;
a group with several copies in what is kept keeps them all. Both list the copies,
their groups and the space reclaimed before asking, and `--dry-run` stops there.

In the GUI, <<cmd "mf:duplicate">> `keep` and `trash` take the query the
[[metarecord-list panel]] shows, or the checked metarecords: build the rule in a
list, read what it holds, then run it (<<key "mf:duplicate keep query" "e q k">>).

!! What follows

The copies go to the [[Trash]], so a mistake is recoverable
([[Restoring from the trash]]); the space is freed when the trash is pruned
([[Trash retention]]).

The group is updated at once — its count, its reclaimable space, and the group
itself, gone once a single copy is left: the trash deletes a copy's metarecord
through the daemon before it moves the bytes, and the daemon re-counts the group
in that same write ([[Duplicate groups]]). The panel then reads the groups again,
keeping the expanded groups and the cursor where they were.

A copy deleted any other way — `mf trash -f`, a file manager — leaves its group
the same way: see [[Duplicate groups]].
